---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Clip from '../../components/Clip.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import {
  capabilityTiers,
  chassisRequirements,
  skidSteer,
  actuators,
  armResults,
  orbitResults,
  orbitConditions,
  architecture,
} from '../../data/fortis';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'fortis');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing project entry: fortis');
---

<CaseStudy
  entry={entry}
  standfirst="A tokamak was never designed to be serviced by a robot, so the vessel fixed every dimension before design began. I led the software, simulation and vision work that turned those fixed dimensions into a drivetrain, an arm and a stack that could be trusted inside them."
>
  <h2>Context and constraints</h2>

  <p>
    DIII-D is a fusion research tokamak at General Atomics. Inspecting and
    servicing the vessel interior is currently manual work in a confined,
    delicate space. The sponsor asked for a tethered robot that could enter the
    vessel, orbit toroidally along it, and carry an arm for camera inspection and
    thermocouple placement on plasma-facing components.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-tokamak-context" eager />

  <p>
    The only way in is the R0 port and a narrow entrance tunnel. They open onto a
    graphite-walled interior with a low-friction floor and a step between the
    diverter and floor planes. Nothing inside may be modified or contacted, which
    rules out fixtures, rails, magnets and anything else that would make the
    problem easier. Every external dimension on the robot was therefore fixed by
    the reactor before the team drew anything.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-r0-port" />

  <p>
    The sponsor expressed the ask as four capability tiers. The fourth was
    descoped to future work by agreement early on, so the program targeted the
    first three.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...capabilityTiers} />

  <h2>Role</h2>

  <p>
    I was the Software, Simulation and Vision Lead, and the overall technical
    lead for the five-person team. I owned the software architecture, the entire
    simulation program, the perception and state estimation stack, the
    containerized development environment and Jetson Orin bring-up. I ran the
    technical direction of the team and the sprint reviews that gated design
    decisions.
  </p>

  <p>
    My teammates owned the mechanical design and CAD, the electrical
    distribution, and fabrication. The chassis and arm drawings on this page are
    their work; the studies that set the numbers on those drawings are mine. The
    hardware results below were produced by the whole team.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements decomposition</h2>

  <p>
    The capability tiers are sponsor language. These are the chassis
    requirements they decompose into, and they are what the simulation program
    was actually run against.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...chassisRequirements} />

  <p>
    One of these is subtler than it looks. The entry cross-section is not
    satisfied by the robot's driving posture: with wheels mounted, the chassis is
    wider than the tunnel. It is satisfied by entering on its side through the
    narrow face and being brought upright after passing through, which is a
    constraint on the entry sequence and on the underbelly geometry rather than
    on the chassis footprint.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-diverter-step" />

  <h2>Design decisions</h2>

  <h3>Drivetrain: X-drive over skid-steer</h3>

  <p>
    The team started on skid-steer, which is the default for a four-wheeled
    ground robot and the cheapest thing to build. To orbit the vessel from its
    landing orientation, a skid-steer robot has to perform a 90 degree point turn
    while straddling the step. I simulated that turn across 60 geometrically
    valid configurations, sweeping wheel radius, wheel width, wheelbase, track
    width and motor torque.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three configurations completed the turn. All three reached 85 to 116 degrees
    of tilt and drifted 7 to 10 inches radially. Next to plasma-facing
    components, a robot that nearly inverts itself and slides ten inches toward
    the wall is a failure regardless of whether the yaw target was met, so the
    honest reading of the study is that zero configurations passed.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...skidSteer} />

  <p>
    The reason is geometric rather than a tuning problem. During a point turn
    every wheel traces an arc around the robot center, and the step is a circle
    those arcs must cross. The wheels therefore have to climb a wall nearly equal
    to their own radius mid-turn, and no combination of wheelbase and track width
    in the buildable range avoids the crossing. The three configurations that
    completed were the smallest wheel, narrowest tire and narrowest track in the
    sweep, which is the corner of the parameter space with the least margin left
    for real conditions. The study also assumed a friction coefficient more
    generous than the measured graphite floor.
  </p>

  <p>
    That redirected the locomotion architecture to an omnidirectional X-drive on
    omni wheels in a chamfered chassis. It won on the primary functional
    criterion independently of the failure above: the mission is a toroidal
    orbit, and an X-drive strafes directly along it with no yaw required to
    translate, so the wheels never cross the step at all. The robot straddles the
    step for the entire orbit instead of turning over it.
  </p>

  <p>
    X-drive beat mecanum, the other holonomic option, on mechanical grounds. Its
    90 degree rollers are simpler and more durable than 45 degree mecanum
    rollers, 8 inch omni wheels were available off the shelf at the size needed,
    and 45 degree corner mounting falls naturally out of a chamfered chassis
    rather than being packaged in against it. Kinematically the two are
    equivalent for this application, so the tie broke on parts availability and
    roller robustness.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-endoskeleton-cad" />

  <h3>Arm reach: 30 inches over 50 inches</h3>

  <p>
    A 50 inch arm covers more of the vessel per chassis position, which is why it
    was the starting point. It lost on actuator budget. At 50 inches the arm
    needed oversized actuators simply to carry its own weight plus the 3 lb
    payload, and those actuators broke both the cost and the mass budget, which
    in turn raised the tipping problem for the chassis carrying them. At 30
    inches the demand stayed inside the torque range of steppers the team could
    afford while still meeting the sponsor requirement, so reach was traded for a
    buildable actuator set.
  </p>

  <p>
    Link material moved from aluminum to carbon fiber for the same reason.
    Aluminium links with external wiring cascaded into larger and costlier
    motors; carbon fiber tubing cut link mass and let the wiring route internally
    through the hollow sections, which removed the snag risk of external looms
    inside a vessel that must not be contacted. Three links collapse to a 16.877
    by 2.744 inch stow profile.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-stow" />

  <h3>Motion timing on a microcontroller, not the host</h3>

  <p>
    Coordinated multi-axis stepping runs on a dedicated microcontroller rather
    than on the compute module. The alternative, stepping from the host, was
    rejected because step timing would then depend on the operating system
    scheduler, and a late step pulse on a geared stepper is a lost position
    rather than a late one. The host owns the analytic inverse kinematics and the
    motion action interface, the microcontroller owns step timing, and the two
    meet at a serial contract with heartbeat and status reporting.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-subsystem" />

  <h2>Simulation methodology</h2>

  <p>
    The budget did not allow physical prototypes. Simulation therefore had to
    close the design loop before anything was fabricated, and the loop was the
    deliverable as much as any individual number: CAD geometry with real masses
    and materials went into a simulation model with modeled wheels and reactor
    floor, out came torque, speed and stability sweeps, and a sprint review gated
    on whether the criteria were met. Geometry did not advance until numbers
    existed for it, and the bill of materials was locked only once the design
    stopped changing under the sweeps.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-validation-loop" />

  <h3>Modelling omni wheels without anisotropic friction</h3>

  <p>
    The physics engine has no anisotropic friction, so an omni wheel cannot be
    modeled directly: there is no way to declare a surface that grips along one
    axis and rolls freely along the other. I built each roller ring as a
    five-sphere chain instead, so the geometry itself produces the free-rolling
    transverse axis rather than a material property doing it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substitution is not free, and characterizing the error mattered more than
    hiding it. Sphere rollers in contact generate more friction than real
    free-spinning rollers, so the model overestimates the torque required to
    move. That means every motor margin the simulation reported is conservative
    by construction: the real robot demands less than the sweep says, never more.
    A model that erred the other way would have been useless for sizing motors.
  </p>

  <h3>Constraint-aware Monte Carlo for the arm</h3>

  <p>
    Sampling joint space blindly wastes almost all the samples, because most of
    the joint space puts the arm through the chassis, through the floor, or
    outside the vessel. I drew 5,000 poses uniformly across the four joints with
    the 3 lb payload applied in every single sample to hold the worst case, then
    filtered: 2,380 poses were rejected for chassis, self or floor collision, and
    a further 606 for leaving the reactor envelope, leaving 2,014 poses that are
    physically reachable inside the vessel.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before trusting any of that I verified the sampler itself was uniform across
    each joint range, because pass rates computed over a biased draw are not pass
    rates.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-mc-sampling" />

  <p>
    Each surviving pose was then evaluated for stability by projecting the total
    center of gravity against the chassis support polygon. The design decision
    inside that check is the chassis mass used: I ran the physics at the lightest
    plausible chassis mass in the design range rather than the expected one,
    because a lighter chassis tips more easily under arm cantilever, so any pose
    that survives at the light mass is safe at the real one.
  </p>

  <p>
    The important structural property is that this filtered set became the shared
    input for both the joint torque analysis and the chassis stability analysis.
    Actuator sizing and tip-over margin were computed against exactly the same
    population of poses, so the two results compose instead of describing two
    different robots.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>

  <h3>Arm</h3>

  <DataTable {...armResults} />

  <p>
    No pose in the reachable set is analytically unstable. Twenty-five of the
    2,014 tipped in physics under the deliberately light chassis mass, all of
    them inside the support polygon analytically, which is dynamic rocking on the
    step rather than a static stability failure.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-poloidal-map" />

  <DataTable {...actuators} />

  <p>
    Every joint peaks below 60 percent of its continuous rating. The shoulder is
    the loaded joint at 17.62 Nm peak against a 30 Nm rating, roughly 1.7 times
    margin at the single worst pose in the reachable set.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-torque" />

  <Clip id="armSim" />

  <h3>Drivetrain</h3>

  <DataTable {...orbitConditions} />

  <DataTable {...orbitResults} />

  <p>
    Worst-wheel 95th-percentile torque reaches 4.10 Nm at 0.30 m/s against 4.98
    Nm of continuous motor capability at 60 A, so the drive stays inside
    continuous rating across the entire commanded speed range with the
    conservative wheel model in place. Peak excursions of 10 to 11 Nm are
    short-duration roller-contact spikes well inside transient capability.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-orbit-torque" />

  <p>
    One detail in that plot is worth reading rather than skimming. The front-left
    wheel trends consistently higher than the other three throughout the sweep,
    because the arm center of gravity sits forward of chassis center. The
    asymmetry is expected and bounded rather than a modeling artifact: the orbit
    runs in a fixed direction, so the load biases onto the front pair and stays
    there. It is also the reason the margin is quoted against the worst wheel
    rather than the mean.
  </p>

  <Clip id="orbitSim" />

  <h2>Implementation</h2>

  <p>
    The stack is a containerized ROS 2 Humble environment bundling the
    middleware, motion planning, the controller framework and a visualization
    bridge, so every team member ran a byte-identical stack regardless of
    machine. That removed environment drift as a category of problem across five
    people, which on a one-term project is worth more than any individual
    feature. Continuous integration builds and tests the workspace on every
    change, and linting runs both locally on commit and in the same pipeline.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...architecture} />

  <h3>Motion is state-gated</h3>

  <p>
    A mission state machine holds nine states and fifteen events, with
    transitions and their guards expressed as a data table rather than nested
    conditionals. It is pure logic with no middleware or hardware dependencies,
    which is what makes it testable in isolation and what let the transition
    table be exercised exhaustively before any of it ran on a robot.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-software-layers" />

  <p>
    Every action component sits above it rather than beside it. The drive
    component rejects any motion command issued outside a permitted mission phase
    and emits an explicit stop signal on rejection rather than silently
    discarding the command, so a rejected command is observable. The arm
    component was scaffolded against the same contract before its kinematics
    existed, which is why inverse kinematics and the motion action dropped in
    later without renegotiating the interface.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two properties matter more than the state count. Any state can transition to
    the fault state through an unguarded wildcard, which is what lets the drive
    health monitor latch a fault from anywhere in the mission. And leaving the
    fault state requires an explicit operator acknowledgement, so recovery is
    never automatic: a drive that comes back healthy on its own does not clear
    the fault, because the condition that caused it has not been inspected.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-mission-states" />

  <h3>Drive path</h3>

  <p>
    Operator teleoperation publishes a body velocity command. An X-drive
    kinematics component maps commanded body motion to four wheel velocity
    setpoints, and those go out over a daisy-chained CAN bus to four controllers
    running onboard velocity control. The kinematics geometry constants are
    pinned to the robot description by a regression test that fails the moment
    either side moves, so the model the simulation used and the code the robot
    runs cannot drift apart quietly.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-block-diagram" />

  <h3>Perception</h3>

  <p>
    Five depth cameras feed the stack: four on the chassis faces through a
    powered USB 3 hub with a single uplink to the compute module, and one on the
    arm. Each camera back-projects its aligned depth into a point cloud in its own
    optical frame, the clouds are transformed into the body frame and merged on a
    fixed-rate timer, and the merged cloud integrates into a persistent voxel
    map.
  </p>

  <p>
    A map saved on one run becomes the reference for the next, and the difference
    between them is published both as visual markers and as a machine-readable
    summary. For an inspection robot whose entire purpose is noticing what
    changed inside a vessel between runs, that difference is the product rather
    than a debugging aid.
  </p>

  <p>
    Visual odometry runs off the front camera and is keyframe-anchored: every
    frame is matched against the current keyframe rather than against the
    previous frame, so per-frame matching noise does not compound between
    keyframe refreshes and drift accrues per keyframe instead of per frame.
    Motion is recovered by lifting matched features through depth and solving for
    pose with outlier rejection.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because the depth cameras cannot stream inside the development container, I
    built a synthetic camera rig that publishes the exact topic contract of the
    real hardware, including aligned depth and inertial data for one to four
    mounts. The whole perception chain is developed and tested against it and
    then verified live on the compute module, which is what makes the perception
    tests runnable by anyone on the team without the robot.
  </p>

  <h3>State estimation</h3>

  <p>
    An Extended Kalman Filter fuses wheel odometry, visual odometry and inertial
    data into a single continuous estimate. The interesting engineering is in
    what is deliberately excluded.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wheel odometry contributes translation only. Its yaw rate is not fused at
    all, because the rear wheels slip under load and a slipping wheel injects a
    phantom yaw rate that the gyroscope never sees, which makes the wheel-derived
    heading actively misleading rather than merely noisy. Visual odometry also
    contributes translation only, because its rotation quality collapses as
    feature count drops while the gyroscope stays uniform. The gyroscope owns yaw
    outright.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither translation source is fused as an absolute position either, only as a
    velocity, because both are open-loop dead reckoning with no absolute
    reference and feeding their absolute position in would inject unbounded drift
    as though it were a measurement. The two fail in different ways, one by slip
    and one by tracking loss, so each is followed according to its own stamped
    covariance and neither is ever trusted for heading.
  </p>

  <h2>Demonstrated</h2>

  <p>
    Holonomic chassis drive under operator command with closed-loop wheel
    velocity, and toroidal orbit motion proved on hardware. Visual odometry
    running across four simultaneous camera streams with inertial data retrieval.
    Commanded arm motion through the reach envelope with object pick and stow,
    and the payload retained through the stow motion.
  </p>

